drm/rockchip: lvds: do not take over a panel bridge's funcs

rockchip_lvds_bind() wraps a panel with drm_panel_bridge_add_typed(), then
sets lvds->panel to NULL because the bridge owns it now. Immediately after,
it overwrites that bridge's ops and funcs with its own -- and its
get_modes() reads lvds->panel, the pointer just set to NULL.

So the connector asks the bridge for modes, the bridge (now wearing this
driver's funcs) asks for lvds->panel, gets NULL, and returns nothing. The
panel's timing never reaches the connector, which falls back to the generic
mode list. On a 1024x600 LVDS panel that means the connector offers 1024x768,
800x600 and friends, and nothing can drive the display correctly.

Measured on a Rockchip RK3126C tablet with a 1024x600 panel. Before:

  rockchip-lvds lvds: panel=4fcc1c99 bridge=00000000 endpoint_id=0 children=1
  rockchip-lvds lvds: get_modes panel=00000000 returned 0
  $ cat /sys/class/drm/card0-LVDS-1/modes
  1024x768
  800x600
  ...

After, the connector offers exactly the panel's own mode:

  $ cat /sys/class/drm/card0-LVDS-1/modes
  1024x600

A panel wrapped by drm_panel_bridge_add_typed() already answers get_modes
from the panel, so leave its funcs alone; only claim them for a bridge this
driver found in the device tree itself.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_lvds.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_lvds.c
index 6baa25f97..79d19966a 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_lvds.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_lvds.c
@@ -664,6 +664,7 @@ static int rockchip_lvds_bind(struct device *dev, struct device *master,
 	int ret = 0, child_count = 0;
 	const char *name;
 	u32 endpoint_id = 0;
+	bool panel_bridge = false;
 
 	lvds->drm_dev = drm_dev;
 	port = of_graph_get_port_by_id(dev->of_node, 1);
@@ -731,6 +732,7 @@ static int rockchip_lvds_bind(struct device *dev, struct device *master,
 	drm_encoder_helper_add(encoder, lvds->soc_data->helper_funcs);
 
 	if (lvds->panel) {
+		panel_bridge = true;
 		lvds->bridge = drm_panel_bridge_add_typed(lvds->panel, DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_LVDS);
 		drm_panel_put(lvds->panel);
 		lvds->panel = NULL;
@@ -741,9 +743,20 @@ static int rockchip_lvds_bind(struct device *dev, struct device *master,
 	}
 
 	if (lvds->bridge) {
-		lvds->bridge->driver_private = lvds;
-		lvds->bridge->ops = DRM_BRIDGE_OP_MODES;
-		lvds->bridge->funcs = &rockchip_lvds_bridge_funcs;
+		/*
+		 * Only claim the bridge's ops when it is a bridge this driver
+		 * found itself. A panel wrapped by drm_panel_bridge_add_typed()
+		 * already answers get_modes from the panel, and taking those
+		 * funcs over here would route the connector back into
+		 * rockchip_lvds_bridge_get_modes(), which reads lvds->panel --
+		 * set to NULL a few lines above. The panel's timing would never
+		 * reach the connector.
+		 */
+		if (!panel_bridge) {
+			lvds->bridge->driver_private = lvds;
+			lvds->bridge->ops = DRM_BRIDGE_OP_MODES;
+			lvds->bridge->funcs = &rockchip_lvds_bridge_funcs;
+		}
 
 		ret = drm_bridge_attach(encoder, lvds->bridge, NULL, DRM_BRIDGE_ATTACH_NO_CONNECTOR);
 		if (ret)
